### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### 12 - MCP SERVER + CLIENT TESTING
###
### Amsha Agent exposes TWO MCP surfaces and one admin API:
###
###   A) MCP SERVER (inbound - Amsha Agent as server, external client calls it)
###      Endpoint: /mcp  (Quarkiverse MCP extension)
###      Also available: /mcp/sse (legacy SSE stream)
###      Protocol: JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP (supports SSE for streaming)
###      Session: Mcp-Session-Id header (captured from MCP-1 response)
###      Handshake: initialize → notifications/initialized → tools/list → tools/call
###      Exposed tools: github, recall_memory_search, archival_memory_search (configured via amsha.mcp.exposed-tools)
###      Allowlist: amsha.mcp.tool-allowlist controls which tools can be called
###
###   B) MCP CLIENT (outbound - Amsha Agent connects to remote MCP servers)
### POST /api/v1/mcp/connect/discover → preview remote tools (throwaway, no persistence)
### POST /api/v1/mcp/connect → request/complete durable connect (tools disabled)
### DELETE /api/v1/mcp/connect/{name} → disconnect + deregister tools
###      GET    /api/v1/mcp/connect        → list connected servers
###      PATCH  /api/v1/mcp/connect/{name} → reconfigure endpoint/credentials (`If-MCP-Revision`)
###      POST   /api/v1/mcp/import/preview → bounded browser multipart preview (no network I/O)
###      POST   /api/v1/mcp/import/apply   → apply one preview entry through normal HITL flow
### Dynamic tools appear only after explicit per-tool enable approval.
###      (The agent tool "register_mcp_server" is the LLM-driven equivalent - see FLOW G;
###       it adds STDIO/Podman transports and an explicit execution category.)
###
###   C) MCP ADMIN (read-only status/tool inspection)
###      GET /api/v1/mcp/status  → enabled flag + dynamic tool count
###      GET /api/v1/mcp/tools   → exposed internal + dynamic tool details
###
### Session ID flow for FLOW B:
###   1. Run MCP-1 (initialize) - sends no Mcp-Session-Id header
###   2. Response header contains: Mcp-Session-Id: <uuid>
###   3. Capture it: @mcpSessionId = {{mcpInit.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}
###   4. All subsequent requests include: Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}
###
### Self-connect smoke test (FLOW D):
###   MCP-CLIENT-1 connects Amsha Agent to itself at http://localhost:8080/mcp.
###   After success: Amsha Agent now has its own tools registered as dynamic tools.
###   MCP-ADMIN-2 shows registeredDynamicTools with ≥1 entry.
###
### Telegram: /mcp reflex shows live MCP status (zero LLM tokens).
###
### Prerequisites: Amsha Agent running in serve mode, amsha.mcp.server-enabled=true (default)
###
### Rate limits: the API key allows ~20 requests/min (10 mutations/min). Running the
### whole file back-to-back triggers 429s - run flow-by-flow, or space requests out.
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@adminBase = http://localhost:8080/api/v1
@mcpBase   = http://localhost:8080
# API key for the running instance. Set this to the value configured via
# amsha.api.keys / AMSHA_API_KEYS (the token used to launch the native binary).
# @validKey  = <your-api-key>
# Secret sent as an outbound MCP Authorization header. Stored ENCRYPTED in the
# vault (SecretVault) and referenced per connection; never logged in plaintext.
# This is the auth credential the REMOTE MCP server expects (its API key / bearer
# token / custom header value) - obtain it from that server's operator or docs.
# Amsha does not mint it; you supply the value and Amsha encrypts + attaches it.
# For a self-connect smoke test any non-empty value works (the local /mcp ignores it).
# @mcpSecret = <remote-mcp-server-token>

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW A - MCP ADMIN (run as health checks before any protocol tests)
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [MCP-ADMIN-1] Check MCP server status
# @name mcpStatus
# Expect 200: {"serverEnabled":true,"dynamicToolCount":0}
# 404 → amsha.mcp.server-enabled=false in config.
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/status
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-ADMIN-2] List all MCP tools - internal + dynamic
# @name mcpToolList
# Expect 200:
# {
#   "exposedInternalTools": [{"name":"github",...},{"name":"recall_memory_search",...},{"name":"archival_memory_search",...}],
#   "registeredDynamicTools": []   ← empty until FLOW D self-connect runs
# }
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/tools
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW B - MCP PROTOCOL (Amsha Agent as server, REST client as MCP client)
### Run strictly in order: MCP-1 → MCP-2 → MCP-3 → MCP-4 → MCP-5
### The Mcp-Session-Id is captured from MCP-1 response and reused.
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [MCP-1] Initialize - start the MCP handshake (NO session ID yet)
# @name mcpInit
# Expect 200: JSON-RPC result with protocolVersion + serverInfo + capabilities
# Response header: Mcp-Session-Id: <uuid> - captured below.
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "initialize",
  "params": {
    "protocolVersion": "2024-11-05",
    "capabilities": {},
    "clientInfo": {
      "name": "rest-client-test",
      "version": "1.0"
    }
  }
}

###

# Capture session ID from MCP-1 response header - must appear between MCP-1 and MCP-2
@mcpSessionId = {{mcpInit.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}

###

### [MCP-2] notifications/initialized - completes the MCP handshake
# Expect 202: empty body (notification has no JSON-RPC response per spec)
# MUST be sent before any tools/list or tools/call request.
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "notifications/initialized",
  "params": {}
}

###

### [MCP-3] tools/list - discover what Amsha Agent exposes
# Expect 200: tools array containing "github" and "recall_memory_search"
# These come from amsha.mcp.exposed-tools via McpToolBridge.
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 2,
  "method": "tools/list",
  "params": {}
}

###

### [MCP-4] tools/call - invoke recall_memory_search
# Expect 200: result.content[0].text = recall_memory search output
# Flow: McpToolsBean.recallMemorySearch() → McpToolBridge.invoke()
#       → ToolRegistry.invokeTool() → recall memory search impl
# Audit event MCP_TOOL_INVOKED persisted for every call.
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 3,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "recall_memory_search",
    "arguments": {
      "query": "Amsha Agent architecture overview"
    }
  }
}

###

### [MCP-5] tools/call - invoke github
# Expect 200: result.content[0].text carries the github output (or an approval
# message). GitHub is a HITL-approved tool, so without an approved session the
# content carries an "requires human approval" message rather than live data -
# still HTTP 200. (JSON-RPC results are always HTTP 200 per spec; isError
# reflects an actual tool failure.)
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 4,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "github",
    "arguments": {
      "operation": "list_repos",
      "paramsJson": "{}"
    }
  }
}

###

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW C - MCP ERROR / EDGE CASES
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [MCP-ERR-1] Call a tool NOT in the allowlist
# Expect 200 HTTP with JSON-RPC error body (per spec, errors are HTTP 200)
# {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":5,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"Invalid tool name: sandbox_exec"}}
# SANDBOX_EXEC was removed from the ToolCapability enum, so any reference to it is
# rejected as an unknown/invalid tool name.
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 5,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "sandbox_exec",
    "arguments": {}
  }
}

###

### [MCP-ERR-2] tools/list without session ID
# With a valid Accept header the server treats this as a fresh connection that
# must start with initialize, so it returns HTTP 200 with a JSON-RPC error:
# {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":6,"error":{"code":-32601,"message":"The first message from the client must be \"initialize\": tools/list"}}
# A 4xx is only returned when the Accept header is missing/invalid (not from the
# missing session id).
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 6,
  "method": "tools/list",
  "params": {}
}

###

### [MCP-ERR-3] notifications/initialized without prior initialize → 404
# Session does not exist - notification is rejected with HTTP 404 (empty body).
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: 00000000-dead-beef-0000-000000000000

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "notifications/initialized",
  "params": {}
}

###

### [MCP-ERR-4] tools/call on unknown tool name
# Expect 200 HTTP with JSON-RPC error body
# {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":7,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"Invalid tool name: does_not_exist"}}
POST {{mcpBase}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
Mcp-Session-Id: {{mcpSessionId}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 7,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "does_not_exist",
    "arguments": {}
  }
}

###

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW D - MCP CLIENT (Amsha Agent connecting OUT to remote servers)
###
### Self-connect smoke test - no external MCP server needed.
### MCP-CLIENT-1 connects Amsha Agent to itself at localhost:8080/mcp.
### After success: dynamic tools are registered in DynamicToolCapabilityRegistry.
### The connect body also accepts an optional "headers" map of auth secrets
### (stored encrypted in the vault); see FLOW F for the secure-header path.
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [MCP-CLIENT-1] Self-connect - Amsha Agent as client to itself
# First response is 202 with taskId; approve it before the handshake.
# The completed response is durable CONNECTED metadata with toolsRegistered=0.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "self",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp"
}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-2] Verify dynamic tools registered after self-connect
# Expect 200: registeredDynamicTools is empty until a discovered tool is enabled
# Both with mcpServerName="self" and transport="HTTP_SSE"
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/tools
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-3] List connected servers
# Expect 200: durable connection row includes state, revision,
# enabledToolCount=0, and discoveredToolCount=N.
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-4] Agent can use dynamic MCP tool
# After self-connect, the agent's tool schema includes the dynamic tools.
# This test exercises the full agent → dynamic tool → MCP client path.
# Requires a configured LLM (the coordinator decides which tool to call).
# NOTE: session creation REQUIRES autonomyLevel (returns 400 otherwise).
# @name mcpAgentSession
POST {{adminBase}}/sessions
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "autonomyLevel": "READONLY"
}

###

POST {{adminBase}}/sessions/{{mcpAgentSession.response.body.sessionId}}/run
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
    "goal": "Search your memory for Amsha Agent architecture details using the memory search tool.",
    "agentRole": "coordinator",
    "maxIterations": 3
}

###

DELETE {{adminBase}}/sessions/{{mcpAgentSession.response.body.sessionId}}
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-5] Disconnect the self server
# Expect 200: {"name":"self","deregistered":true,"toolsRemoved":N}
# After this: MCP-ADMIN-2 shows registeredDynamicTools: []
DELETE {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect/self
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-6] Verify dynamic tools cleared after disconnect
# Expect 200: registeredDynamicTools: []
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/tools
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-7] Connect to an external MCP server (edit url and uncomment)
# POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
# Content-Type: application/json
# X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
#
# {
#   "name": "remote",
#   "url": "http://your-mcp-server:3000/mcp"
# }

###

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW F - SECURE OUTBOUND CONNECTION (header secret management)
###
### The enhanced POST /api/v1/mcp/connect accepts an optional "headers" map.
### Each header value is stored as an ENCRYPTED SECRET in the vault
### (SecretVault -> @WorkspaceScoped VaultKeyCache, scoped per user) and tracked
### by McpConnectionHeaderSecretRefDto. On connect/reconnect the secret is
### resolved and attached to outbound HTTP requests to the remote server.
###
### Validation (McpConnectionLifecycleService.validateHeaders):
###   - header names must match the RFC 7230 token subset; transport-owned /
###     forbidden names (content-type, host, mcp-session-id, connection, ...) are
###     rejected.
###   - header values containing ${, $( or backticks are rejected (no injection).
###   - duplicate header names are rejected.
###
### Vault ops run inside WorkspaceScopeHolder so the workspace-scoped key cache
### is reachable from the REST request thread. Without it the connect 500s with
### "WorkspaceScoped context was not active". Connections + secret refs survive
### restart; the lifecycle service reconnects enabled HTTP connections and
### re-resolves secrets (audit: MCP_CREDENTIAL_CONFIGURED / _REMOVED).

### [MCP-CLIENT-SEC-1] Secure connect with an Authorization header secret
# Expect 202 approval, then durable CONNECTED metadata with toolsRegistered=0.
# Proves: McpConnectResource -> lifecycleService.persistSuccessfulHttp()
#   -> validateHeaders() -> storeHeaderSecrets() -> secretVault.store()
#   (wrapped in WorkspaceScopeHolder). Audit: MCP_CREDENTIAL_CONFIGURED.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "secure",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp",
  "headers": {
    "Authorization": "Bearer {{mcpSecret}}"
  }
}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-SEC-2] Verify dynamic tools registered for the secure server
# Expect 200: registeredDynamicTools contains tools with mcpServerName="secure"
GET {{adminBase}}/mcp/tools
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-SEC-3] Disconnect the secure server (also purges the vault secret)
# Expect 200: {"name":"secure","deregistered":true,"toolsRemoved":N}
# Vault secret removal is audited MCP_CREDENTIAL_REMOVED.
DELETE {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect/secure
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-SEC-ERR-1] Connect with forbidden header (content-type) -> 400
# validateHeaders() rejects transport-owned header names.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "forbidden",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp",
  "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" }
}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-SEC-ERR-2] Connect with injection-prone header value -> 400
# containsUnsafeHeaderValue() rejects ${, $( and backticks.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "injection",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp",
  "headers": { "X-Evil": "$(curl evil)" }
}

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW E - MCP CLIENT ERROR / EDGE CASES
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [MCP-CLIENT-ERR-1] Connect with missing url → expect 400
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "no-url"
}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-ERR-2] Connect to unreachable server → expect 502
# McpClientService throws McpConnectionException after timeout.
# McpConnectResource maps it to HTTP 502.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "dead",
  "url": "http://localhost:9999/mcp"
}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-ERR-3] Disconnect server never connected → expect 404
DELETE {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect/never-connected
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-ERR-4] Connect duplicate server name → overwrite (HTTP 200)
# Connecting to "self" again while it is already connected OVERWRITES the existing
# registration: HTTP 200 with toolsRegistered: 0 (tools are already registered).
# Use a distinct name to avoid clobbering an active connection.
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "name": "self",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp"
}

###

### Disconnect again (cleanup after ERR-4)
DELETE {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect/self
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-CLIENT-ERR-5] Connect without API key → expect 401
POST {{adminBase}}/mcp/connect
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "unauth",
  "url": "http://localhost:8080/mcp"
}

###

### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### FLOW G - AGENT-FACING MCP REGISTRATION (register_mcp_server tool)
###
### The agent tool "register_mcp_server" is the PRIMARY user-facing way to add an
### MCP server. Unlike the admin REST /api/v1/mcp/connect (HTTP/SSE only, fixed
### NETWORK category - see FLOW D/F), this tool also supports:
###   - STDIO transports (local process via command/args/env, incl. Podman-managed)
###   - per-header auth secrets (same encrypted vault storage as FLOW F)
###   - an explicit execution CATEGORY: NETWORK | COMPUTE | DATABASE | INTERNAL
### It is invoked by the coordinator through natural language, so it REQUIRES a
### configured LLM and a non-READONLY session (SUPERVISED/FULL) with the tool
### granted to the role. There is no direct REST endpoint for it.
###
### Tool parameters (from GET /api/v1/tools):
###   name*      unique server name (alphanumeric / _ / -)
###   transport* http | stdio
###   url        HTTP/SSE endpoint, e.g. http://host:3000/mcp
###   headers[]  [{name, value}] auth headers (stored encrypted, like FLOW F)
###   command    STDIO: binary to run (npx, python, docker)
###   args       STDIO: comma-separated arguments
###   env        STDIO: KEY=VALUE lines
###   category   NETWORK | COMPUTE | DATABASE | INTERNAL
###
### Example chat goal (LLM-driven):
###   "Register an MCP server named 'local' over http at http://localhost:8080/mcp
###    with category NETWORK."
### This yields the same dynamic-tool registration as FLOW D/F, plus the stdio /
### podman and category-routing options the REST endpoint does not expose.

### [MCP-REG-1] Confirm the register_mcp_server tool is registered
# Expect 200: the tool list contains "register_mcp_server".
GET {{adminBase}}/tools
Accept: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [MCP-REG-2] Register via agent (LLM-driven, needs SUPERVISED+ session)
# Create a non-readonly session, then ask the coordinator to register the server.
# Requires a configured LLM and the tool granted to the role.
# @name mcpRegSession
POST {{adminBase}}/sessions
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "autonomyLevel": "SUPERVISED"
}

###

POST {{adminBase}}/sessions/{{mcpRegSession.response.body.sessionId}}/run
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

{
  "goal": "Register an MCP server named 'local' over http at http://localhost:8080/mcp with category NETWORK.",
  "agentRole": "coordinator",
  "maxIterations": 3
}

###

DELETE {{adminBase}}/sessions/{{mcpRegSession.response.body.sessionId}}
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### MCP lifecycle approval notes
# POST /mcp/connect and POST /mcp/connect/discover return 202 with taskId.
# Approve with POST /mcp/connect/approvals/{taskId}/approve.
# Discovery is throwaway; connect persists all discovered tools disabled.
# POST /mcp/connect/{name}/tools/{toolName}/enable also returns an approval task.
# GET /mcp/connect/{name}/tools lists persisted descriptions and schemas.
# Lifecycle mutations may send If-MCP-Revision; stale values return HTTP 409.
